tle="Discuss this template">t</abbr></a></li><li class="nv-edit"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:BBC_sidebar" title="Special:EditPage/Template:BBC sidebar"><abbr title="Edit this template">e</abbr></a></li></ul></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p id="mwJg">The <b id="mwJw">British Broadcasting Corporation</b> (<b id="mwKA">BBC</b>) is a British <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_service_broadcaster" title="Public service broadcaster" class="mw-redirect" id="mwKQ">public service broadcaster</a> that serves as the primary national public broadcasting company of the United Kingdom, headquartered at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadcasting_House" title="Broadcasting House" id="mwKg">Broadcasting House</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London" title="London" id="mwKw">London</a>, England. Originally established in 1922 as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Broadcasting_Company" title="British Broadcasting Company" id="mwLA">British Broadcasting Company</a>, it evolved into its current state with its current name on 1 January 1927. It is the oldest and largest local and global broadcaster by stature and by number of employees, with a total staff of 21,000.</p>

<p id="mwLQ">The BBC was established under a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_charter#United_Kingdom" title="Royal charter" id="mwLg">royal charter</a>,<sup about="#mwt36" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-crown_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"crown"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-crown-2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-crown-2" id="mwLw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">]</span></span></a></sup> and operates under an agreement with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secretary_of_State_for_Culture,_Media_and_Sport" title="Secretary of State for Culture, Media and Sport" id="mwMw">secretary of state for culture, media and sport</a>.<sup about="#mwt39" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNw">]</span></span></a></sup> Its work is funded principally by an annual <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Television_licensing_in_the_United_Kingdom" title="Television licensing in the United Kingdom" id="mwOA">television licence fee</a><sup about="#mwt42" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TrustAR_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TrustAR"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TrustAR-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TrustAR-4" id="mwOQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwOg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPA">]</span></span></a></sup> which is charged to all British households, companies, and organisations using any type of equipment to receive or record live television broadcasts or to use the BBC's streaming service, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPlayer" title="IPlayer" class="mw-redirect" id="mwPQ">iPlayer</a>.<sup about="#mwt45" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TVLic_5-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TVLic"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TVLic-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TVLic-5" id="mwPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The fee is set by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_government" title="British government" class="mw-redirect" id="mwQg">British government</a>, agreed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_United_Kingdom" title="Parliament of the United Kingdom" id="mwQw">Parliament</a>,<sup about="#mwt48" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TVLic2_6-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TVLic2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TVLic2-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TVLic2-6" id="mwRA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRw">]</span></span></a></sup> and is used to fund the BBC's radio, TV, and online services covering the nations and regions of the UK. Since 1 April 2014, it has also funded the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_World_Service" title="BBC World Service" id="mwSA">BBC World Service</a> (launched in 1932 as the BBC Empire Service), which broadcasts in 43 languages as of 2026.<sup about="#mwt51" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-7" id="mwSQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSw">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwTQ">Some of the BBC's revenue comes from its commercial subsidiary <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_Studios" title="BBC Studios" id="mwTg">BBC Studios</a> (formerly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_Worldwide" title="BBC Worldwide" id="mwTw">BBC Worldwide</a>), which sells BBC programs and services internationally and also distributes the BBC's international 24-hour English-language news services <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_News_(international_TV_channel)" title="BBC News (international TV channel)" id="mwUA">BBC News</a>, and from BBC.com, provided by BBC Global News Ltd.<sup about="#mwt54" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwUQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUw">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt57" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-9" id="mwVQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwVg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVw">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwWQ">Since its formation on 18 October 1922, the BBC has played a prominent role in British life and culture.<sup about="#mwt60" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-10" id="mwWg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXA">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXQ">]</span></span></a></sup> It is sometimes informally referred to as <b id="mwXg">the Beeb</b> or <b id="mwXw">Auntie</b>.<sup about="#mwt63" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Jackson_11-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jackson"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Jackson-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Jackson-11" id="mwYA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwYQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYg">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt66" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-12" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-12" id="mwZA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZw">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1923 it launched <i id="mwaA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_Times" title="Radio Times" id="mwaQ">Radio Times</a></i> (subtitled "The official organ of the BBC"), the first broadcast listings magazine; the 1988 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas" title="Christmas" id="mwag">Christmas</a> edition sold 11 million copies, the biggest-selling edition of any British magazine in history.<sup about="#mwt69" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RadioTimes_Oldest_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RadioTimes Oldest"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-RadioTimes_Oldest-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RadioTimes_Oldest-13" id="mwaw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

